Epistemic bubbles created by algorithmic personalisation reduce exposure to cross-cutting viewpoints, increasing political polarisation — Pew Research shows polarisation in the US tracked social media adoption closely from 2012.
The speed of social media prevents the verification processes that traditional journalism employs — rumours become 'facts' before correction, and corrections never reach the same audience as the original falsehood.
Deepfakes and AI-generated content, distributed through social media, are making it increasingly impossible to verify the authenticity of evidence — a structural epistemological threat to democratic deliberation.
